PUBLIC RESPONSE: Minimum compensation set up for an n-plex is n single colour compensation controls plus one negative / bead-alone control.

For the 5 fluorochromes you list you need

* 1 x Streptavidin bead – bead only / unstained – for background/autofluorescence and to set the negative boundary in each detector;
* 1 x AF350-conjugated biotinylated reagent captured on streptavidin beads;
* 1 x GFP-conjugated biotinylated reagent captured on streptavidin beads;
* 1 x PE-conjugated biotinylated reagent captured on streptavidin beads;
* 1 x AF647-conjugated biotinylated reagent captured on streptavidin beads;
* 1 x AF750-conjugated biotinylated reagent captured on streptavidin beads.

That is 5 single-stain compensation controls and 1 unstained bead control, 6 tubes in total, which is the absolute minimum required to build a spectral compensation matrix and to define the negative population for the sort.

In practice for a real sort the single-stain set is complemented with rigorous gating controls. The sorting-relevant set is normally

* the 1 unstained/bead-alone control,
* the 5 single-stain compensation beads, and
* 5 Fluorescence Minus One controls, one for each of AF350, GFP, PE, AF647 and AF750, to place gates on real cells/beads.

That is 11 tubes in routine high-quality practice, the 6 compensation/negative tubes being the strictly essential minimum.

